prompt:
The morning bake is out and the shift has to sign off the batch before it goes to the shop floor.

Already fixed and not yours to move: three loaves from the one bake are lying on the cooling rack, each with its numbered batch tag clipped to the rail below it, 1, 2 and 3; the curved lame the baker cut them with hangs at the head of the rack with its blade face turned towards the oven; the product card is propped beside it. The oven is at one end of the room and the shop door at the other, and both are in the frame.

What the product card carries:
tags 1 and 2 are the long loaves, cut with a run of shallow slanting cuts;
tag 3 is the round loaf, cut with a straight-down cross;
a shallow slanting cut lifts a flap on the side the blade leans towards, and the flap bakes into an ear on that side;
on a long loaf each cut is started before the one in front of it has finished, so the run of cuts overlaps along the loaf.

Which way the ears end up facing and how the cuts end up lying along each loaf is the part I am deliberately not telling you, and no loaf from an earlier bake is on the rack to copy. Nothing is written on the rack or on a loaf beyond the batch tags and that card.
